    "tldr": [
      "WhatsApp restricts numbers when people block or report them, or when sending looks automated: sudden bursts of identical messages to strangers.",
      "Send to people who know you, personalise every message, space messages out, start small and make it easy to opt out.",
      "For large lists or transactional messages, use the official WhatsApp Business Cloud API instead of a personal number."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Why do WhatsApp numbers get banned for bulk messaging?",
        "a": "Mostly because recipients block or report the number, or because the sending pattern looks like spam: many identical messages, sent fast, to people who never saved your number."
      },
      {
        "q": "How many WhatsApp messages can I send per day without a ban?",
        "a": "There is no published safe number. What matters more is who you send to and how they react. A warm list of customers can receive far more messages safely than a cold list of strangers."
      },
      {
        "q": "Does adding a delay between messages prevent bans?",
        "a": "It helps, because it avoids sudden bursts, but it is not a guarantee. NexBotix sends with a 3 to 5 second gap, which works best combined with consent and personalisation."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is the WhatsApp Business API safer for bulk messages?",
        "a": "Yes. The official Cloud API is designed for business messaging at scale, uses approved templates and is the right choice for large lists and transactional messages."
      },
      {
        "q": "What should I do if my number gets restricted?",
        "a": "Stop sending, review who you messaged and how, and follow WhatsApp’s instructions in the app. When you resume, send only to your warmest contacts and slow down."
      }
    ],
