    "slug": "step-by-step-guide-automating-otp-delivery-whatsapp-api",
    "title": "How to Send OTP Codes on WhatsApp with an API: A Step-by-Step Guide",
    "metaTitle": "Send OTP Codes on WhatsApp with an API (Step by Step) | NexBotix",
    "metaDescription": "Send one-time passwords over WhatsApp from your backend: generate and store codes safely, call the NexBotix API, handle errors and know when to use the Cloud API.",
    "excerpt": "WhatsApp is a natural place to deliver login and verification codes. This guide shows how to generate OTPs safely, send them through the NexBotix API, and build a fallback.",
    "content": "<p>Codes sent by SMS are slow in some countries, cost more than people expect and are easy to miss. Many users already have WhatsApp open, which makes it a convenient channel for login and verification codes.</p>\n<p>This guide shows the full flow: generating the code safely, sending it through the NexBotix API, verifying it, and planning for failures.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-does-whatsapp-otp-delivery-work\">How does WhatsApp OTP delivery work?</h2>\n<p>The flow has four steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The user enters their phone number in your app.</li>\n<li>Your server generates a one-time code, stores a hash of it with an expiry time, and sends the code to WhatsApp through the API.</li>\n<li>The user types the code into your app.</li>\n<li>Your server checks the code against the stored hash, the expiry time and the attempt count, then marks it as used.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The messaging part is one HTTP request. The security lives in steps 2 and 4, on your server.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-1-get-an-api-key\">Step 1: Get an API key</h2>\n<p>Create an API key in the NexBotix <strong>Developer Hub</strong>. Keys start with <code>nex_</code>. Store it in an environment variable, never in your front-end code:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">NEXBOTIX_API_KEY=nex_your_key_here\n</code></pre>\n<p>Make sure your WhatsApp number is connected in the dashboard. Messages are sent from it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-2-generate-and-store-the-code-safely\">Step 2: Generate and store the code safely</h2>\n<p>Use a cryptographically secure random generator, not <code>Math.random()</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">import { randomInt, createHash } from &#39;node:crypto&#39;;\n\nconst code = String(randomInt(0, 1_000_000)).padStart(6, &#39;0&#39;);\nconst codeHash = createHash(&#39;sha256&#39;).update(code + process.env.OTP_PEPPER).digest(&#39;hex&#39;);\n\nawait db.otp.upsert({\n  phone,                      // e.g. &quot;919876543210&quot;\n  codeHash,\n  expiresAt: Date.now() + 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes\n  attempts: 0,\n});\n</code></pre>\n<p>Store the hash, the expiry and an attempt counter. Never log the plain code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-3-send-the-code-with-the-api\">Step 3: Send the code with the API</h2>\n<p>Send a single message with <code>POST /api/v1/send</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">const res = await fetch(&#39;https://nexbotix.online/api/v1/send&#39;, {\n  method: &#39;POST&#39;,\n  headers: {\n    &#39;Content-Type&#39;: &#39;application/json&#39;,\n    &#39;X-API-Key&#39;: process.env.NEXBOTIX_API_KEY,\n  },\n  body: JSON.stringify({\n    phone,\n    message: { text: `Your verification code is ${code}. It expires in 5 minutes. Don&#39;t share it with anyone.` },\n  }),\n});\n\nif (!res.ok) throw new Error(`OTP send failed: ${res.status}`);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Phone numbers are digits with the country code and no <code>+</code> or spaces. Keep the message short and include the expiry and a “don’t share” warning.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-4-verify-the-code\">Step 4: Verify the code</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-js\">const record = await db.otp.find({ phone });\nif (!record || record.expiresAt &lt; Date.now()) return fail(&#39;Code expired&#39;);\nif (record.attempts &gt;= 5) return fail(&#39;Too many attempts&#39;);\n\nconst hash = createHash(&#39;sha256&#39;).update(input + process.env.OTP_PEPPER).digest(&#39;hex&#39;);\nif (hash !== record.codeHash) {\n  await db.otp.update({ phone, attempts: record.attempts + 1 });\n  return fail(&#39;Incorrect code&#39;);\n}\n\nawait db.otp.delete({ phone }); // one-time means one time\nreturn ok();\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"step-5-protect-the-endpoint\">Step 5: Protect the endpoint</h2>\n<p>OTP endpoints are a favourite target for abuse, because each request sends a message on your account. Add:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rate limits per phone number and per IP</strong>, for example one code per minute and a handful per hour.</li>\n<li><strong>A CAPTCHA or similar check</strong> on the form that requests codes.</li>\n<li><strong>Resend cooldowns</strong> in your UI, so impatient users don’t trigger five codes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"step-6-plan-for-failures\">Step 6: Plan for failures</h2>\n<p>No channel delivers 100% of the time. Handle errors from the API, show the user a clear message, and offer a fallback after a short wait: “Didn’t get it? Send by SMS or email instead.” The NexBotix email API (<code>POST /api/v1/email/send</code>) works with the same API key if email is your fallback.</p>\n<h2 id=\"personal-number-or-whatsapp-business-cloud-api\">Personal number or WhatsApp Business Cloud API?</h2>\n<p>Sending OTPs from a number connected by QR code is quick to set up and fine for testing, internal tools and small volumes.</p>\n<p>For a production login flow, use the <strong>WhatsApp Business Cloud API</strong>. Meta provides authentication message templates for exactly this purpose, delivery is designed for business volumes, and your personal number isn’t at risk. See the trade-offs in our <a href=\"/blog/bulk-whatsapp-messaging-best-practices-avoid-bans\">bulk WhatsApp guide</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prefer-to-let-an-ai-agent-send-messages\">Prefer to let an AI agent send messages?</h2>\n<p>If you’re building with Claude, Cursor or Windsurf, the <a href=\"/blog/whatsapp-mcp-server-control-whatsapp-from-ai-agents\">WhatsApp MCP server</a> exposes <code>send_whatsapp_message</code> and scheduling tools to your agent. For code-triggered messages like OTPs, the REST API above is the right fit. Full reference: <a href=\"/docs\">API docs</a>.</p>\n",
